     1	---
     2	github: https://github.com/hachej/boring-ui/issues/1123
     3	issue: 1123
     4	state: needs-owner-approval
     5	updated: 2026-08-07
     6	flag: flag:BORING_ENV_MOUNTS (server-side; exec grants default-deny regardless)
     7	track: owner
     8	---
     9	
    10	# gh-1123 — executable environments: per-agent fs + exec grants
    11	
    12	## Problem
    13	
    14	Agents get exactly one executable root. The Workspace+Sandbox pairing
    15	invariant realizes the primary `user` filesystem in Bash; every other
    16	filesystem binding is deliberately file-tools-only
    17	(`docs/PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT_MODEL.md`, "Environment Mount: separate execution
    18	realization"). The owner-ratified direction (2026-08-07, #970 review): an
    19	agent should have access to **various filesystems AND execution, configurable
    20	per agent** — e.g. a client agent with its own readonly `knowledge/` fs plus a
    21	scratch exec workspace, grants-configured per client.
    22	
    23	Today the single-root assumption is hard-coded end to end:
    24	
    25	- `SandboxProviderCreateContextV1` takes one `workspaceRoot`; no mount list
    26	  (`packages/boring-sandbox/src/shared/providerV1.ts`).
    27	- `buildBwrapArgs` emits exactly one `--bind <root> /workspace --chdir
    28	  /workspace` (duplicated byte-identical in `packages/boring-sandbox/src/
    29	  providers/bwrap/` and `packages/boring-bash/src/agent/runtime/`).
    30	- Every `RuntimeBashStrategy` spawn hook takes one root; `buildBwrapArgs`
    31	  always emits `--chdir /workspace` (the `local-sandbox` strategy's
    32	  `sandboxRoot` option feeds only `bwrapSpawnHook`'s env).
    33	- `RuntimeFilesystemBinding` (`packages/agent/src/server/runtime/mode.ts`)
    34	  has no `mountable`/materialization field and no `sourceRoot` — bindings are
    35	  pure logical file-ops, and #970's multi-root readonly projection engine is
    36	  a *virtual* union with no single real directory to bind.
    37	- No grants vocabulary exists in code: zero hits for
    38	  `environment.bash.execute` anywhere. The per-agent enforcement seam exists
    39	  (`createAgentHostRuntimeCapabilityProjection.resolveBinding`, fed by the
    40	  #1114 fleet/policy config on main), but nothing rides it yet.
    41	
    42	## Solution
    43	
    44	### The Environment abstraction
    45	
    46	An **Environment** is the leased execution realization an agent binding runs
    47	in. It already half-exists: `EnvironmentLease` (`packages/agent/src/server/
    48	agent-host/environmentLease.ts`) refcounts one provider-created
    49	`WorkspaceSandboxPairV1` per resolved scope. This epic widens what a lease
    50	realizes, without breaking the pairing invariant:
    51	
    52	```text
    53	Environment = primary workspace root            (unchanged, pairing invariant)
    54	            + mount set: N extra roots          (each an authorized binding:
    55	              at logical paths, ro/rw            direct real dir OR live view)
    56	            + exec capability                    (explicit per-agent grant)
    57	            + lease lifecycle                    (refcounted lease, keyed by
    58	                                                 mount set; #1083 attaches later)
    59	```
    60	
    61	**Lease keying (the load-bearing decision).** `EnvironmentLeaseManager`
    62	keys records by `[workspaceScopeId, placementIdentity]` and shares one
    63	sandbox across agent bindings. If mounts stayed per-agent inputs to a
    64	shared sandbox, an ungranted agent leasing after a granted one would see
    65	its mounts — a grant bypass. Therefore: the **resolved mount set is part of
    66	the lease identity** (folded into `placementIdentity`). Agents whose
    67	grants resolve to different mount sets get different environments; sharing
    68	happens only on identical mount sets. Grant enforcement is structural, not
    69	advisory.
    70	
    71	**Reclamation.** Today `EnvironmentLeaseManager.release()` only decrements
    72	`references`; on the common path a zero-reference record is never disposed
    73	(disposal happens only via `retire()`/`close()`). Acceptable with one
    74	record per workspace scope; unacceptable once mount sets fork records —
    75	every distinct grant-set would strand a live sandbox until host shutdown,
    76	and grant edits would strand the old record forever. This epic adds
    77	disposal-at-zero (or idle-timeout eviction with an explicit bound) for
    78	mount-forked records. Owner decision 5 (fork-over-fail) is ratifiable only
    79	together with this reclamation story.
    80	
    81	**Fingerprint reconciliation.** `createEnvironmentProvisioningFingerprint`
    82	documents that contribution grants are deliberately excluded so grant-only
    83	changes share the lease (`runtimeScopeIdentity.ts:61-65`). The mount set is
    84	not a grant: it is Environment-mutating identity derived *from* grants.
    85	It enters `placementIdentity` (the lease key), and therefore flows into the
    86	fingerprint too, which already digests `placementIdentity` — key and
    87	fingerprint stay consistent by construction. The invariant's wording is
    88	amended, not violated: grant changes that leave the resolved mount set
    89	unchanged still share the lease. `AGENT_SHARED_ENVIRONMENT_UNAVAILABLE`
    90	keeps its current meaning (same-key fingerprint mismatch); differing mount
    91	sets never reach it because their keys already differ.
    92	
    93	Rules (amending `PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT_MODEL.md`, not repealing it):
    94	
    95	1. `mount requires authorization` stays the only valid direction. A mount is
    96	   derived from an existing filesystem binding the agent already holds; the
    97	   mount adds physical visibility, never new logical access.
    98	2. Two mount kinds (owner direction 2026-08-07): `mountKind: 'direct'`
    99	   binds a static real directory (`materialization.sourceRoot` — knowledge/,
   100	   fixtures; zero-cost path); `mountKind: 'view'` mounts a **live
   101	   Operations→FUSE bridge** over a virtual/computed binding. No copy/sync
   102	   engine is ever built — a view mount routes every read through the
   103	   adapter at access time.
   104	3. Exec is an explicit per-agent grant (`environment.bash.execute`), never
   105	   inferred from read/write access. Default deny.
   106	4. Readonly bindings mount `ro`; a binding never mounts wider than its
   107	   file-ops access. View mounts are readonly in v1 (EROFS on write).
   108	5. Actor-varying resources never enter a **shared** execution namespace —
   109	   unchanged. They may now mount because environments fork per
   110	   agent/grant-set (lease keying below): each environment's view mount
   111	   evaluates as exactly its agent's identity, never a blended one.
   112	
   113	### View mounts: the Operations→FUSE bridge
   114	
   115	A small FUSE filesystem whose `read`/`readdir`/`getattr` callbacks invoke
   116	the `RuntimeFilesystemBinding` operations vtable live, mounted on the host
   117	and bind-mounted into the bwrap sandbox at `/mnt/<fsid>`. Properties:
   118	
   119	1. **Adapter stays the policy enforcement point.** Every bash read routes
   120	   through the same operations the file tools use — the "mount requires
   150	#1087 per-agent policy seam) gains:
   151	
   152	- `environment.bash.execute` — scoped per filesystem id: the agent may run
   153	  commands with that filesystem's mount visible/selected. Grant on the
   154	  primary `user` fs reproduces today's behavior.
   155	- `environment.mount.manage` — reserved name only in this epic (see
   156	  Non-goals).
   157	
   158	Enforcement points (two, both structural):
   159	
   160	1. **Mount computation** — environment scope resolution: the agent's grants
   161	   resolve to a mount set *before* the lease is acquired; the set enters the
   162	   lease key and the provider create context. An ungranted agent's
   163	   environment simply never contains the mount.
   164	2. **Tool assembly** — `buildAgentComposition` (which unconditionally
   165	   includes bash via `buildHarnessAgentTools` today) gates bash-tool
   166	   presence on the exec grant. `resolveBinding` only returns the
   167	   already-built composition, so the gate lives in composition, not there.
   168	
   169	v1 gates **the bash tool only**. Other exec-shaped surfaces
   170	(`SandboxProvisioningOperationsV1.exec`, provisioning hooks,
   171	`runtimeScope.extraTools`, pi extensions) are not gated by this grant;
   172	they are host-configured, not agent-requested, and mounts are already
   173	absent from ungranted agents' environments via point 1. This scoping is
   174	stated, not hidden. `filesystem.read/write`, `git.*`, `network.egress`,
   175	`secret.use` from the grants design are out of scope here (bindings + #971
   176	already govern fs access).
   177	
   178	### Composition with existing seams
   179	
   180	- **Binding contract**: `RuntimeFilesystemBinding` gains an optional
   250	
   251	## Decisions
   252	
   253	- **Environment = lease + mount set + exec grants**, realized inside the
   254	  existing single `SandboxProviderV1.create` call. No second realization
   255	  path, no new top-level runtime object.
   256	- **Mountability is a declared capability of a binding**
   257	  (`materialization.sourceRoot` present), never inferred from probing.
   258	- **Default deny**: no exec grant → no change from today except the primary
   259	  fs, which is implicitly granted for compat (recorded, not silently
   260	  special-cased: fleet policy compiler materializes the implicit grant).
   261	- **Mount set is lease identity**: grants → mount set → lease key. Two
   262	  agents share an environment only when their resolved mount sets match;
   263	  otherwise the lease manager forks environments (no
   264	  `AGENT_SHARED_ENVIRONMENT_UNAVAILABLE` hard-fail for mount-set
   265	  differences — see owner decision 5).
   266	- **bwrap-first**: v1 providers = bwrap (full support); direct/host,
   267	  vercel-sandbox, and remote-worker reject mounts (capability
   268	  `mounts: false`). Pure/readonly modes have no exec at all — unchanged.
   269	- **Mount namespace**: `/mnt/<fsid>`, never under `/workspace` (see owner
   270	  decision 3 for confirmation — it is agent-visible surface).
   271	- **ro-only named mounts in v1**: `access: 'rw'` is contract-supported but
   272	  only the primary root uses rw. rw views are out of scope entirely
   273	  (owner-ratified); rw direct mounts are a follow-up issue, not a v1 gate.
   274	- **Two mount kinds, one provider contract**: `mountKind: 'direct' |
   275	  'view'`; the view bridge lives above the provider seam and hands it an
   276	  ordinary host directory.
   277	- **FUSE dependency**: `@cocalc/fuse-native` (maintained fork) with the
   278	  sidecar/9p fallback recorded as owner-visible risk (view-bridge section,
   279	  property 6).
